    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on some walnut cabinet builds and im having a bit of an issue with the dovetails. the pins are coming out a bit ragged at the tips—lots of tiny splinters and fiber tear-out right at the ends. i've been using a standard bevel on my chisels but i can't figure out why it's happening even though they feel sharp. any advice on how to fix the grain tear-out or if i should be changing my bevel angle?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ragged tips on dovetail pins, especially with walnut, are a classic sign of the grain fighting back, which is often more stubborn than a truly dull chisel. It's less about the sharpness and more about how the wood fibers are being *cut* versus *torn*. You might need to adjust your attack angle, or perhaps your chisel bevel is too aggressive for the density of that walnut. Trying to carve through the grain with a standard bevel can lead to tear-out, so you might need to approach it more like a very careful, precise scraping motion, or perhaps even switch to a slightly more acute bevel angle to shear the fibers cleanly.",
      "source": "target",
