    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i'll check the humidity in the shop. one more thing though—is there any specific way i should be prepping the wood surface or the chisel itself to prevent this? like, should i be using a specific sharpening stone or maybe a different type of honing compound to get that extra clean shear on the walnut fibers so they don't just snap?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For that extra clean shear, the stone and compound are crucial; you need a finish that truly *slices*, not just scrapes. If you are using a standard sharpening stone, consider moving up to a finer grit stone, perhaps 4000 grit or higher, for the final honing stage. A good honing compound can help polish the very edge, creating a microscopic bevel that allows the chisel to glide through the fibers rather than catching them. Think of it like making sure your edge is polished down to the molecular level so it can glide through the wood like a perfectly honed, razor-sharp blade.",
